WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.030
- Is it kind of like, the whole scene,

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:04.260
is it kind of bittersweet
that it's coming to an end?

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.910
- Definitely, mostly it's
sad because I can't stalk

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:10.230
Ted Danson any more, every
summer, although will it stop me?

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:11.093
No.

00:00:11.093 --> 00:00:11.926
(laughter)

00:00:11.926 --> 00:00:14.500
But we end the show so beautifully,

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.310
and it's so rare that
the creators get a chance

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.840
to make the call on when you say goodbye.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.240
Normally, a network just pulls the plug,

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.800
and so it's amazing to have
planned this year in advance

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.990
and soak up all of what
makes it beautiful.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:30.070
And it's such a good ending,
everyone's gonna cry,

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:31.663
no one's gonna be okay.

00:00:31.663 --> 00:00:32.556
(audience laughing)

00:00:32.556 --> 00:00:33.389
- [Kelly] It's awesome.

00:00:33.389 --> 00:00:34.542
I love crying.
- [Jamel] We're all doomed.

00:00:34.542 --> 00:00:36.450
- So I can't believe I
found out this is actually,

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.040
The Good Place is your first acting job,

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.470
'cause you, I know you were
hosting in England before,

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.382
but this is your first,
that's a hell of a first job.

00:00:43.382 --> 00:00:45.081
- I didn't sleep with anybody to get this.

00:00:45.081 --> 00:00:46.175
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:00:46.175 --> 00:00:47.112
(laughter)

00:00:47.112 --> 00:00:50.274
- Did you say I didn't sleep
with anyone? (laughing)

00:00:50.274 --> 00:00:52.139
- I didn't sleep with anyone to get this,

00:00:52.139 --> 00:00:53.676
if anything that would've lost me the job.

00:00:53.676 --> 00:00:54.570
- [Kelly] Oh my god, I
would hang out with you.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:55.564
- I'm not great.

00:00:55.564 --> 00:00:58.372
(laughter)

00:00:58.372 --> 00:00:59.205
- That was awesome.

00:00:59.205 --> 00:01:01.080
- The audition was my
first ever time acting,

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.010
and so I went in, and I think
maybe because I was so sure

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:06.610
I wouldn't get the job, I had
that kind of reckless abandon,

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:08.250
of just a maniac on the loose,

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.112
and he took that as confidence,

00:01:11.112 --> 00:01:13.140
which was a mistake, and gave me the job.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.512
- Which was a mistake.
- Yeah, here I am.

00:01:15.512 --> 00:01:18.134
(audience cheering and clapping)

00:01:18.134 --> 00:01:20.700
- I love though that you
also promote body positivity,

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.990
you have a campaign, a
movement, called I Weigh, right?

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:24.844
- Yeah, it's a body neutrality movement.

00:01:24.844 --> 00:01:27.535
(audience cheering and clapping)

00:01:27.535 --> 00:01:29.630
- So why is this important to you?

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:31.560
- So, I Weigh is a body
neutrality movement,

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.780
it's about getting away from
thinking about your body

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.610
and instead, thinking about
the things in your life

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:36.730
that you should be grateful for,

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:38.660
like, I weigh the sum off all my parts,

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:40.870
I weigh, like, you can't measure my worth

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:43.080
on a weighing scale, you can't
measure your own like that.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.150
It's insane that we still do this to women

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:46.170
in this day and age.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:49.500
So it's about, you know,
I weigh my relationship,

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.300
my contribution to society,
my financial independence,

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:53.133
my activism.

00:01:53.133 --> 00:01:55.270
That's what I weigh, that's my worth,

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:56.428
and so it's a movement thing.

00:01:56.428 --> 00:01:58.161
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:01:58.161 --> 00:02:00.040
- That is very cool.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.930
- And it's this whole global movement

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:04.700
where we have almost a million followers

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.500
and we get five or 6000 more a day

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.520
if people from everywhere,
from all walks of life,

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:12.050
who are just tired of being
undermined and underestimated

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:14.540
by society, and it's this
really beautiful space,

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.350
that I'm launching into
a company next year

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:19.010
and we're gonna take
this as far as we can.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:20.010
And we're changing laws,

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:21.973
and changing global policies together,

00:02:21.973 --> 00:02:24.289
and we're just done
with how we treat women.

00:02:24.289 --> 00:02:25.197
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:02:25.197 --> 00:02:29.170
- I think it's important,
because I think sometimes

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:32.320
we focus so much on the aesthetics
of what health looks like

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.490
instead of actual health,

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:35.950
and what's going on inside your body,

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:37.140
and we just don't know, you know?

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.580
So it's like, what's going
on in someone's life,

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:41.750
so I think it's a really
important thing you're doing,

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.660
especially 'cause you have
such a big spotlight on you,

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:45.110
so thank you for doing that.
- Thanks.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:46.600
Thanks.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.230
You've been through some
body shaming before.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.310
- I have no idea what you're
referring to (laughs).

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:53.892
I literally am getting
trolled right now, online.

00:02:53.892 --> 00:02:55.033
- That's so wild,

00:02:56.186 --> 00:02:58.190
but I remember you going through that

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:00.700
and then very shortly afterwards
I gained a lot of weight

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:02.820
back in England, I was
a radio DJ by the way,

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:03.905
you couldn't even see me,

00:03:03.905 --> 00:03:07.260
people just wanted to know I
was thin over the airwaves,

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.940
so I got nationally fat
shamed for six months

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:13.510
in my own country, and it
was your response to yours

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:15.670
that gave me the strength
to get through mine.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:17.736
- Did I use a word that
I can't say on this show?

00:03:17.736 --> 00:03:19.680
- [Jamel] Yeah (laughs).
- Yeah.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.010
It is a great campaign though,

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:22.460
and thank you so much for doing it.

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:23.940
It's important, like I said,
'cause you've a spotlight,

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:25.330
it's important for all
of us who have spotlights

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:26.500
to do something really cool,

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:29.070
outside of our egocentric lives, you know?

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:30.790
You can catch Jamel though,

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:34.320
hosting the series The
Misery Index, Tuesdays on CBS

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.960
and also watch her in the final
episode of The Good Place,

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.303
Thursdays on NBC.

